  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1211 AM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nWe'll see a zonal to northwesterly upper level flow regime for the \nnext several days, which is a rather more common flow regime for \nthis time of year. That translates into a mostly dry pattern with \nback and forth transitions between modified Pacific to Canadian \nairmass. Surface high pressure overhead this morning pushes off to \nthe east later today, with a tightening pressure gradient and \nincreased southerly winds across central/north central South Dakota. \nRRFS BUFKIT profiles mixed winds top out around 25kts for \nPierre/Mobridge, with the more mixing prone GFS closer to \n30kts. Thats about where the NBM tops out. \n\nWarm advection ahead of a northwest flow wave Saturday could support \nmid level clouds with some shallow skinny CAPE. NBM POPs are about \n20%, however CONSSHORT is closer to 30 to 50% which better aligns \nwith coverage/intensity of reflectivity depicted by the CAMS. \n\nSurface high pressure moves overhead later Sunday morning, with most \nof Saturday night featuring gradient flow thus limiting frost \npotential.  For Sundays lows, the 25th-75th range in temperatures \nhas a relatively low spread at 3 to 6F and bottoms out in the low \n40s. \n\nAs that high moves off, another tight gradient develops across the \nDakotas, somewhere on the order of 8 to 14mb across the state. NBM \nwinds are on the order of 25 to 30kts. The front that follows will \nlikely be dry as the wave and low are well to the north.",
